  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 21,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.451,
            0.069,
            0.817,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "I’m Up and Down, and Round and Round"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.844,
            0.068,
            0.884,
            0.085
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "113"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.107,
            0.886,
            0.178
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Similarly, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle  BCD"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is half the angle subtended by the arc BAD at O. This is the angle denoted by the double arc BOD. Since C is on arc BCD, we need to move from OB to OD via A. So, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle  BCD = \\frac{1}{2} ( \\angle  BOD  )"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.181,
            0.734,
            0.212
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm{S}0,\\angle \\mathrm{BAD}+\\angle \\mathrm{BCD}=\\frac{1}{2}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (complete angle at the centre O)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.212,
            0.503,
            0.232
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A complete rotation at O is "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "3 6 0 ^ { \\circ } ,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.239,
            0.588,
            0.271
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Hence, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle BAD + \\angle BCD = \\frac{1}{2} \\times (360^{\\circ}) = 180^{\\circ}."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.273,
            0.743,
            0.294
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "So, the opposite angles of a cyclic 4-gon add up to "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "1 8 0 ^ { \\circ } ,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.301,
            0.59,
            0.322
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The converse of this theorem also holds."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.336,
            0.885,
            0.396
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Exercise:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " A cyclic quadrilateral has angles measuring "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle A = 80^{\\circ}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle \\mathrm{B} = 110^{\\circ}, \\angle \\mathrm{C} = 100^{\\circ}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle \\mathrm{D} = 70^{\\circ}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . Can such a quadrilateral be drawn? Explain why or why not."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.408,
            0.886,
            0.468
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Theorem 12:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " If two opposite angles of a quadrilateral add up to "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "I 8 0 ^ { \\circ } ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " then the vertices of the quadrilateral lie on a circle, i.e., they are concyclic."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.478,
            0.445,
            0.709
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 10,
              "bbox": [
                0.131,
                0.478,
                0.445,
                0.709
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_21_image_10.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 11,
              "bbox": [
                0.245,
                0.711,
                0.325,
                0.729
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 5.29"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.455,
            0.497,
            0.825,
            0.519
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Given:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " ABCD is a 4-gon (Fig. 5.29)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.456,
            0.522,
            0.689,
            0.543
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_21_equation_13.jpg",
          "content": "\\angle BAD + \\angle BCD = 180^{\\circ}."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.457,
            0.548,
            0.683,
            0.568
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_21_equation_14.jpg",
          "content": "\\angle ABC + \\angle CDA = 180^{\\circ}."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.455,
            0.573,
            0.778,
            0.593
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "To show that:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " ABCD is cyclic."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.454,
            0.598,
            0.877,
            0.715
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Why is this true?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Suppose ABCD is not cyclic. Since A, D, B are not collinear, there is a circle passing through the three points. If that circle does not pass through C, there are two cases: C is outside or C is inside the circle."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.743,
            0.885,
            0.802
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We deal only with the first case and leave the other to the reader. Let E be the point where CD meets the circle (see Fig. 5.29). Then ABED is a cyclic 4-gon. So from Theorem 11, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle BAD + \\angle BED = 180^{\\circ}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.802,
            0.58,
            0.819
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We are also given "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle BAD + \\angle DCB = 180^{\\circ}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.82,
            0.884,
            0.86
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "So "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle BCD = \\angle BED"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . But this is not possible as "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle  BED"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is the exterior angle of BEC and hence greater than "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle  BCE _{ z }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " i.e., greater than "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle  BCD"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.859,
            0.551,
            0.879
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "So, points A, B, C, D all lie on a circle."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
